<article class="env-remark" id="DCADB9">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/DCADB9">Remark <span data-tag="DCADB9">3.1.3.9</span>.</a> The definition of the Cartesian model structure on $(\mSet )_{/S}$ is not self-opposite. Consequently, we can define another model structure on $(\mSet )_{/S}$ as follows: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(C)$"><p>
The cofibrations in $(\mSet )_{/S}$ are precisely the monomorphisms. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(W)$"><p>
The weak equivalences in $(\mSet )_{/S}$ are precisely the <em>coCartesian equivalences</em>equivalence!coCartesiancoCartesian equivalence: that is, those morphisms $f: \overline{X} \rightarrow \overline{Y}$ such that the induced map $f^{op}: \overline{X}^{op} \rightarrow \overline{Y}^{op}$ is a Cartesian equivalence in $(\mSet )_{/S^{op}}$. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(F)$"><p>
The fibrations in $(\mSet )_{/S}$ are those morphisms which have the right lifting property with respect to every morphism satisfying both $(C)$ and $(W)$. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
  <p> We will refer to this model structure on $(\mSet )_{/S}$ as the <em>coCartesian model structure</em>.coCartesian model structuremodel category!coCartesian </p>
</article>